# XSH pthread_atfork()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The handlers only record into static storage and write() to a pipe
# (async-signal-safe), as the page requires in a child of a multi-threaded
# process. The function is obsolescent but still required.

assertion  XSH.pthread_atfork.001
anchor     functions/pthread_atfork.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_atfork.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       After two registrations (A then B) that return 0, fork() runs
           the prepare handlers before it forks in the opposite order of
           registration (B, A), the parent handlers in the parent in
           registration order (A, B) and the child handlers in the child
           in registration order (A, B); the parent handlers run after
           the prepare handlers.
test       t/001-order.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_atfork.002
anchor     functions/pthread_atfork.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       A null address for any of the three handlers is accepted
           (pthread_atfork() returns 0) and only the handlers given run:
           a registration with a prepare handler only, one with a parent
           handler only and one with a child handler only each run just
           that handler.
test       t/002-null-handlers.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_atfork.003
anchor     functions/pthread_atfork.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       The handlers run in the context of the thread that called
           fork(): with fork() called from a created thread, the prepare
           and parent handlers see that thread's ID from pthread_self(),
           and the child handler runs once in the child.
note       Whether the replica of the calling thread in the child has the
           same thread ID is not said, so the child handler's ID is not
           compared.
test       t/003-calling-thread.c
